    "title": "Children's Day",
    "category": "stories",
    "content": "On the bright, crisp morning of November 14th, a witty teacher named Colson found his students looking unusually downcast. Hoping to lift their spirits, he turned toward them and grabbed their attention.\n\n\"Good morning, students! I know you are all bored and exhausted after your examinations, aren't you?\" Colson asked with a gentle smile.\n\n\"Yeah, sir, indeed we are. We are bored and exhausted after the examination; it doesn't even feel like Children's Day,\" one student replied.\n\nColson nodded thoughtfully. \"Hmm, that being said, let me narrate to you the story of a man who had never been happy and never enjoyed his childhood. If you are ready to listen, I would convey the story to you.\"\n\nThe students readily and eagerly agreed, and the teacher began to narrate.\n\nOn a cold, foggy day, a noble man named David was walking through his hometown. It had been decades since he last visited, and as he crossed a street, a vivid flash of memory suddenly sparked in his mind.\n\nHe visualized two boys playing together at a park alongside his younger self\u2014and soon, David recognized them as none other than his childhood friends. In the memory, he watched his younger self take his bike and ride eagerly toward the park. Once he reached it, a strange, unusual sensation washed over his mind. In the vision, the young David suddenly turned, looked elsewhere, and recollected a moment when he and his father were together.\n\n\"David, it is already late!\" his father's voice echoed in the memory. \"Now stop playing. We have to reach school before 8:00, so get on the bike.\"\n\nWith that sudden thought snapping him back to reality, older David felt a heavy wave of exhaustion\u2014yet there was something deeply fascinating about the memory. He sat down on a wooden bench, watching other children play. The joyous scene made him realize just how stressful his own life had been, and he felt a deep ache for the joy he had missed in himself for the past twenty-one years.\n\nAfter some time, the sky turned dark, and heavy water droplets began to fall. Seeking shelter from the rain at a nearby tea shop to keep himself from getting wet, David paused to rest. Exhausted after a long, busy adventure, sipping a hot cup of tea felt simple, yet the warmth and emotion it conveyed were spectacular. As the rain began to settle outside, David felt refreshed, listening quietly to the local radio playing in the background.\n\nOnce the rain stopped, he took his bike and rode on to visit his old school. The moment he arrived, his mind filled with peace and calm. It was already 4:00 PM, and the final bell rang. David watched students rushing out to go home, and after some time, parents arrived to pick them all up. Soon, the campus became completely still and silent.\n\nDavid walked into the management office, where he recognized one of his old friends working as a teacher.\n\n\"Hey, Tim! It's so nice to see you,\" David said, stepping over. \"How are you? How long have you been working here?\"\n\nTim looked up, surprised and delighted. \"Hey, David! It has been years since we last met. You were always so busy abroad\u2014and even during our school days, you were so studious and did not enjoy even a single moment. Sorry, I forgot to inform you how long I have been working here; it has been almost five years now.\"\n\nSitting down together, David and Tim shared a heartfelt discussion that eventually helped relieve David from the stress of his life abroad. Continuing their amusing conversation, the two walked together to Tim's house.\n\n\"It is one of my best moments ever in my life, and I would never forget this,\" David said warmly to Tim.\n\nTim smiled and looked at his friend. \"You had wasted your childhood, David, but make your present memorable and pleasant. Come to my house often\u2014we can make our lives truly precious.\"\n\nDavid nodded with quiet resolve. \"Of course, I would come. I feel bad for my past, but I am resolute that my life from here onwards will be filled with joy and pleasure.\"\n\nTeacher Colson paused his story, looking around the classroom at his engaged students.\n\n\"That is the end of the story,\" Colson said softly.\n\nOne of the students raised a hand and asked, \"Now, is David happily living?\"\n\nColson smiled warmly. \"From then on, David spent his life happily. From this story, we can understand that we must enjoy our childhood happily, as those are the most precious moments in our lives. So enjoy your childhood\u2014and enjoy your Children's Day!\"\n\nAfter that day, the students enjoyed each and every moment of their lives, while learning wholeheartedly for their academics too.",
