    "title": "Lighthouse at the end of the time.",
    "category": "stories",
    "content": "Every night at 11:59, a lighthouse appeared on the horizon.\n\nMara was the only one who saw it.\n\nShe lived on a cliff above the sea, where the old lighthouse had been dark for thirty years. The village said it was haunted. Mara\u2019s grandfather, who had built it, said it was broken. Before he died, he gave her the brass key to its locked lantern room.\n\n\u201cDon\u2019t go up there after midnight,\u201d he told her.\n\nThen he smiled, as if he\u2019d just told a joke.\n\nFor years, Mara obeyed. She watched the distant light blink once, twice, three times\u2014and vanish at midnight exactly.\n\nOn the night the sea froze, she took the key upstairs.\n\nThe lantern room smelled of salt and burnt sugar. At its center stood a little brass door that hadn\u2019t been there before. Mara turned the key. The door swung open onto a staircase spiraling down into darkness.\n\nAt the bottom waited a boy with wet hair and a paper crown.\n\n\u201cYou\u2019re late,\u201d he said.\n\n\u201cLate for what?\u201d\n\n\u201cFor saving the moon.\u201d\n\nHe pointed through a round window. Beyond it, the sea was not frozen. It stretched beneath a sky thick with stars\u2014and the moon was sinking into the water, trailing silver sparks.\n\nMara looked back at the boy. \u201cWho are you?\u201d\n\n\u201cJonah. I was the lighthouse keeper before your grandfather.\u201d\n\n\u201cThat was a hundred years ago.\u201d\n\n\u201cTime gets tangled around here.\u201d He handed her the paper crown. \u201cThe moon fell once before. Someone has to guide it home.\u201d\n\nMara almost laughed. Then she saw the moon\u2019s reflection shudder. A black shape moved beneath it, vast as a mountain.\n\n\u201cWhat happens if it reaches the bottom?\u201d\n\n\u201cNight forgets how to end.\u201d\n\nThe lighthouse\u2019s beam swept across the water. Wherever it touched, the black shape recoiled. Mara understood then: the light wasn\u2019t meant for ships. It was a path, stretched across impossible distance.\n\n\u201cBut the lamp is broken.\u201d\n\nJonah nodded toward the crown. \u201cIt needs a name.\u201d\n\nMara stared at him. \u201cA name?\u201d\n\n\u201cEverything lost needs someone to call it back.\u201d\n\nThe black shape rose. The water climbed the window.\n\nMara thought of her grandfather\u2019s hands, forever smelling of brass polish. She thought of the village asleep beneath the cliff, trusting the dark to pass. Then she thought of the moon\u2014lonely, luminous, slipping away.\n\n\u201cCome home,\u201d she said.\n\nThe lighthouse blazed.\n\nIts beam crossed the sea and wrapped around the moon like a golden thread. The black shape shrieked without sound. The moon climbed the thread, higher and higher, until it settled back into the sky.\n\nThe window filled with dawn.\n\nWhen Mara blinked, she stood alone in the lantern room. The brass door was gone. In her hands lay the paper crown, damp with seawater.\n\nOutside, the old lighthouse shone for the first time in thirty years.\n\nAnd far above it, the moon gave one small, silver wink.\n\nThe next night, at 11:59, Mara saw a light on the horizon.\n\nThis time, it blinked three times.\n\nThen waited.",
